Armada on the AYN Odin / Odin Pro (Snapdragon 845)
==================================================

Unlike Armada's SM8550+ devices, the original Odin boots Linux through
U-Boot: the stock Android bootloader (ABL) chain-loads a U-Boot image from
one of the Android A/B boot slots, and U-Boot provides UEFI to boot Armada's
GRUB from this SD card. Android stays on the other slot — switch slots to
switch OS.

U-Boot images in this folder (from armada-packages/uboot-odin, based on
https://github.com/sigmaris/u-boot 'odin' branch):

  uboot-ayn-odin.img     Odin / Odin Pro (and Odin Base)
  uboot-ayn-odin-m2.img  Odin M2 only

The Odin Lite is MediaTek-based and is NOT supported.

Which install method applies depends on your ABL. Power off and hold
VOL- (+ POWER) to reach the green START/fastboot screen and look for a
`DEFAULT BOOT` line:

  - NO `DEFAULT BOOT` line  -> older ABL that chain-loads the boot slot;
    use Method A (boot slot).
  - `DEFAULT BOOT` present  -> AYN multiboot ABL that boots a `loader`
    PARTITION, not the boot slot; use Method B (loader partition). This
    is what current Odins ship with; Method A will NOT work on them (the
    ABL never chain-loads boot_b in Custom mode -- it just drops to
    fastboot).

Tethered test boot first (works on both; nothing flashed, SD inserted):

     fastboot boot uboot-ayn-odin.img

If U-Boot appears and boots Armada from SD, make it permanent per your ABL:

Method A -- older ABL, boot-slot chainload:

  1. fastboot getvar current-slot   (assume "a"; Linux -> slot "b")
  2. fastboot erase dtbo_b          (else Android's dtbo overlays break
                                     U-Boot's device tree)
  3. fastboot flash boot_b uboot-ayn-odin.img
     fastboot --set-active=b
     fastboot reboot

Method B -- AYN multiboot ABL (`DEFAULT BOOT` present), loader partition:

  The ABL boots a `loader` partition when DEFAULT BOOT = Custom. Stock
  layout has none, so we repartition to add it (this WIPES internal
  Android incl. persist; Armada on the SD is untouched). Uses Project
  Valhalla's odin_custom_boot package (github.com/ProjectValhalla) for the
  repartition GPT -- pick gpt0_64/128/256 by storage size:

  1. fastboot getvar partition-size:userdata
       0xB3B1F3000  -> 64GB  -> gpt0_64.bin
       0x1A209F3000 -> 128GB -> gpt0_128.bin
       0x37EB9F3000 -> 256GB -> gpt0_256.bin
  2. fastboot flash partition:0 gpt0_<size>.bin   (creates loader/efi/rootfs)
  3. fastboot flash loader uboot-ayn-odin.img     (our U-Boot IS the loader)
  4. On the START screen, set DEFAULT BOOT = Custom via "Switch Default
     Boot" (VOL to navigate, POWER to select) if not already.
  5. Cold boot: power off, unplug USB, power on.

  Notes: `fastboot getvar partition-size:loader` may report not-found even
  when the flash succeeded (stale cache) -- trust the OKAY on `flash
  loader`. If U-Boot is rejected from `loader`, flash Valhalla's
  odin-loader-m0.img (a Qualcomm UEFI LinuxLoader) there instead and boot
  Armada's GRUB from the `efi` ESP.

Switching between Armada and Android:

  Method A: boot to fastboot, `fastboot --set-active=a` (Android) or `=b`
  (Armada).
  Method B: on the START screen toggle DEFAULT BOOT between Custom (Armada)
  and Android.

Going back to stock:

  Method A: only the Linux slot's boot/dtbo were changed (inactive Android
  kernel); `fastboot --set-active=a` restores Android, reflashing stock
  boot/dtbo from AYN's firmware fully reverts it.
  Method B: internal Android was repartitioned/wiped; restore with Valhalla's
  restore_odin_m0.sh (flashes the stock gpt0.bin + system + persist) or
  reflash AYN's full firmware package.

Notes:

  - Screen support currently targets the V1 panel (Innolux TD4328). V2-screen
    units may show no display under Linux yet.
  - The /KERNEL file used by SM8550+ devices is not used on the Odin; the
    EFI folder on this SD card is the boot path.
